@@ -187,7 +187,7 @@ The `SpecificationsDataFormat` controls the format of the machine-readable speci
 | `GenerateSpecificationsData` | `bool` | `true` | When `false`, skips generating the specifications data file (YAML/JSON/XML). |
 | `GenerateTestRunReportData` | `bool` | `true` | When `false`, skips generating the test run report data file. |
 | `CompressTestRunReportPayloads` | `bool` | `true` (`false` before 4.0.0) | When `true` (and the data format is JSON), a captured body or a diagram's PlantUML source of 8,192 characters or more (512 in 4.0.0 and 4.0.1) is written in `TestRunReport.json` as `{"$h", "$n", "$z"}` in place of its text, unless that would not make it smaller: `$z` is the base64 of the gzip of the text's UTF-8, `$h` its `b:` address and `$n` its length. A file holding one declares `formatVersion` 2. `kronikol query`, `kronikol merge` and the skill's `query.py` from 3.35.0 read it as they read the plain form; an older `kronikol` refuses it and says to upgrade. See [[Generated Reports › Compressed payloads|Generated-Reports#compressed-payloads-3350]] (3.35.0, #85; the default becomes `true` at 4.0.0). **Obsolete from 5.0.0** (warning CS0618), and removed in v6: it applies only to the `TestRunReport.json` a run writes with `CompressTestRunReport = false`, since a `.krkl` holds every payload as text inside a file compressed whole. |
-| `GenerateMergeableData` | `bool` | `false` | When `true` (and the data format is JSON), enriches `TestRunReport.json` with everything needed to reconstruct a full HTML report later — component relationships, internal-flow data, whole-test-flow fragments, CI metadata and the run's diagnostics — so several files can be combined with `kronikol merge`, which since 3.1.0 writes the merged data file beside the merged HTML. See [Merging Parallel Reports](Merging-Parallel-Reports). The file is larger than the standard report. |
+| `GenerateMergeableData` | `bool` | `false` | When `true` (and the data format is JSON), enriches the data file (`TestRunReport.krkl`, or `TestRunReport.json`) with everything needed to reconstruct a full HTML report later — component relationships, internal-flow data, whole-test-flow fragments, CI metadata and the run's diagnostics — so several files can be combined with `kronikol merge`, which since 3.1.0 writes the merged data file beside the merged HTML. See [Merging Parallel Reports](Merging-Parallel-Reports). The file is larger than the standard report. |
 | `GenerateTestRunReportSchema` | `bool` | `false` (`true` in 4.x) | When `true`, writes the data file's schema beside it: `TestRunReport.schema.json` for a JSON or `.krkl` data file, `TestRunReport.schema.xsd` for XML. Nothing in Kronikol reads it; it is there for a consumer who validates the data file, or the JSON `kronikol decode` writes. `kronikol merge --schema` and `kronikol ingest --schema` ask for it from a command line. |
 | `GenerateSpecificationsMarkdown` | `bool` | `false` | When `true`, writes `<YamlSpecificationsFileName>.md` — the specification as prose, with no run data at all. Unlike its two siblings it is **not** blanked on a failed run. Suppressed by `ExpectedTestCount` like every other specification output. See [Generated Reports](Generated-Reports#specificationsmd). Since 3.1.0. |
 | `GenerateCtrfReport` | `bool` | `false` | When `true`, writes `ctrf-report.json` — the run in [Common Test Report Format](https://ctrf.io), for CI tooling that already reads it. Carries failure messages and traces verbatim, and is swept up by `PublishCiArtifacts` like every other `.json` in the folder. See [Generated Reports](Generated-Reports#ctrf-reportjson-optional). Since 3.1.0. |
